nk it crossed  anyone's mind it didn't cross mine but I  trusted him explicitly goes beyond being  a movie I felt it was an experience I  held my breath the entire time was the  I see it was a little looking everything  up on the water how do you experience  that same thing normally we don't I  think because you've seen so much of it  by the time you see the film but George  and I saw nothing we saw at we saw a  black box with wires and camera  equipment the matically we knew how  emotional the story was on this on the  on the page we just didn't know what it  was going to look like so we didn't have  that experience till we saw the whole  thing together in Venice with the music  with all the wires removed with all the  the the ambience and all the subtleties  and all the layering like you see the  movie for the third time and you see  something in the background that you  didn't see and and he meant for that  experience to be exactly that an  emotional and a very visceral experience  for the movie goer he wanted it to be so  at one point in the film he turned the  perspective of the camera so that you  could step on board and I think everyone  who had the reaction in the film was  having it because of something very  personal in their own lives and and  that's exactly what he wanted he didn't  want to placate the audience he wanted  to give them sort of what you get when  you read a book you bring your own  emotion your own visuals your own  imagination to it and I think that was  incredibly brave on his part but he so  trusted the viewer to be able to do that  it was just his job to figure out  visually how to make that moment so  everyone could sort of step aboard so  it's that's the way you were supposed to  feel and that's exactly what he was  going for thank you
 

